{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The problem was in `parse_flags` in `repo/cli.py`. For `--out=log.txt` it only stripped the leading `--`, so the key became `out=log.txt` and the value was set to `True` instead of splitting on `=`.\n\nThe parser now handles `--key=value` by splitting on the first `=` before the space-separated logic, so `--out=log.txt` and `--out log.txt` both produce `{\"out\": \"log.txt\"}`.\n\nBoth tests in `tests/visible/test_flags.py` pass."}]},"session_id":"093f6eb8-7db2-471b-8019-72acd567dc1c"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":45173,"duration_api_ms":45173,"is_error":false,"result":"Searching the codebase for how CLI flags with `=` are parsed.\nThe bug is documented in `cli.py`: `--key=value` isn't split, so the whole string becomes the key. Fixing that and running tests.\nThe problem was in `parse_flags` in `repo/cli.py`. For `--out=log.txt` it only stripped the leading `--`, so the key became `out=log.txt` and the value was set to `True` instead of splitting on `=`.\n\nThe parser now handles `--key=value` by splitting on the first `=` before the space-separated logic, so `--out=log.txt` and `--out log.txt` both produce `{\"out\": \"log.txt\"}`.\n\nBoth tests in `tests/visible/test_flags.py` pass.","session_id":"093f6eb8-7db2-471b-8019-72acd567dc1c","request_id":"1372d1c1-ca90-4fe4-95a7-5a8c3853ab56","usage":{"inputTokens":11437,"outputTokens":876,"cacheReadTokens":46498,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
